dsh: reasoning:
Now I understand worker-086's "MISMATCH" verdicts. Looking closely, they're mostly methodological artifacts of its own comparator:

- SRC-004: ledger year 2025 = Annals journal publication year (Ann. of Math. 202(2), 2025); arXiv v1 2017. The ledger title says "The interior of dynamical vacuum black holes I: The C^0-stability..." and arXiv fetched title is the same with $C^0$ (LaTeX). So the "year mismatch" is a convention issue (journal year vs arXiv submission year) — the ledger's venue explicitly says "arXiv v1 2017". Actually not a mismatch. Also excerpt similarity 0.022 — worker-086's excerpt similarity metric is broken (probably comparing ledger excerpt with source-label prefixes / LaTeX to plain abstract). Hmm, but the abstract of arXiv v1 might differ from ledger excerpt? Ledger excerpt: "We prove that for all such data, the maximal Cauchy evolution can be extended across a non-trivial piece of Cauchy horizon..." Fetched abstract head: "We initiate a series of works where we study the interior of dynamical rotating vacuum black holes without symmetry. In the present paper, we take up the problem starting from appropriate Cauchy data... We prove that for all such data, the maximal Cauchy evolution can be extended across a non-trivial piece of Cauchy horizon as a Lorentzian manifold with continuous metric." Wait — the fetched abstract HEAD does contain "We prove that for all such data..." So excerpt IS present in the fetched abstract; worker-086's similarity metric (0.022) is broken for math-heavy text (LaTeX/unicode differences). So SRC-004 excerpt mismatch is likely a false positive from a bad similarity metric.

- SRC-033: fetched abstract head mentions "The strength of the blue-shift instability..." and ledger excerpt is a substring "...by slightly relaxing the smoothness assumption on initial data, we are able to prove the analogue..." The fetched Crossref abstract presumably contains that sentence too. So again false positive likely.

- SRC-025: ledger excerpt vs fetched abstract — ledger excerpt says "We establish a 'mass inflation/ridigity' dichotomy. More precisely, we prove assuming (sufficiently slow) decay ... CH_{i+} = D union S ...". Fetched abstract head says exactly "we obtain a \"mass inflation/ridigity\" dichotomy. More precisely, we prove assuming (sufficiently slow) decay of the charged scalar field on the event horizon, that the Cauchy horizon emanating from time-like infinity is $CH_{i^+}= D \cup S$". So the excerpt IS in the fetched abstract; again false positive due to LaTeX/unicode normalization (CH_{i^+} vs $\mathcal{CH}_{i^+}$, "union" vs \cup).

So worker-086's three hard-failure MISMATCHes are likely artifacts of a fragile string-similarity comparator, not real ledger defects. This is EXACTLY the kind of thing a bounded independent worker should check and report: an independent replication with a better comparison method (token-level containment of the excerpt in the fetched abstract after normalization, plus year-convention handling). The HANDOFF explicitly says: "almost all of it was caught by a control rather than by thinking harder" and "run the null first".

So my task: "W021-L1-REPLICATION-01: independent re-fetch replication of worker-086's three MISMATCH findings at frozen L1 hash 315c1914, using a different fetch path (arXiv API export for arXiv rows; Crossref for DOI rows) and an excerpt-containment test with explicit normalization; include a negative control (synthetic corruption must be detected) and a positive control (the same test applied to a known-good row)."

This is class-bound (classes AF-SCC-C0/C2 for SRC-004/025/033, and L1's class list). It's high value: it either (a) refutes worker-086's hard failures → unblocks G-LIT criterion (the controller would otherwise treat them as findings), or (b) confirms them with better evidence. Either outcome is progress. Falsifier: if the normalized excerpt-containment test finds the excerpt absent from the primary record AND field values disagree beyond convention, the ledger defect is confirmed (i.e., my null hypothesis is "no defect").

